ts³11100668879³1version³13³1cache_oldmajor³18³1cache_oldauthor³18³1cache_diff_default_author³11³1revision³19³1text_default³1ip³2205.231.31.86³2ts³21100668879³2version³21³2name³2text_default³2data³2summary³3*³3text³3= Secession =

Lots of secession talk around the world. Some are violent, some not. For more on particular ones, see [[Secession Attempts]].

&nbsp;

<b>Non-Violent Secession</b>

As secession is mostly thought of as the violent kind, non-violent quitting the culture or quitting the state, i.e., how to withdraw as an individual, can be found under [[Education]] and [[Evolution]].

Also, for non-violent secession of groups, see [[Society and Community]], where virtual can mean a society within a society and for Ancappers, an anti-state society within a society with a state.

&nbsp;

<b>Violent Secession</b>

"Secession is the right of every American." Reference the Declaration of Independence or most any of the Founders. But that 'right' was closed out with the Civil War, or the War of Northern Aggression as it is called in some parts. In any case, the closure is still US-politically accepted. So, secession without violence from the US is mostly a dead letter. For attempts at revival of the idea, see as well Secession Attempts.

<b>ZAP and Secession.</b> Ancap remains divided. Some argue that secession entails violence and automatically must violate, even void, ZAP. Others suggest it is a matter of self defense. See [[ZAP and Self Defense]].

Current Law. "Anyone advocating or attempting the illegal overthrow of the United States Government...." It's illegal&#151;their laws, their courts, and their police. And ultimately, their armed forces. This is not a helpful idea, IMO, despite its being done successfully and quite illegally in the Revolutionary War. For those who might try anyway&#151;Keep your powder dry & avoid strict followers of ZAP.

&nbsp;

